  "prompt_text": "The figure compares key climate-driven processes affecting Antarctic and Greenland ice sheets. In both cases, atmospheric influences such as teleconnections alter winds, snowfall, and surface melting, while ocean heat reaches the ice margins from below. The main message is that ice loss is controlled by linked atmosphere–ocean–ice interactions rather than by one factor alone.\n\nFor Antarctica, the diagram emphasizes floating ice shelves and their vulnerability to both surface and basal processes. Surface melting can form ponds and trigger hydrofracturing, while snow accumulation and winds also shape conditions. Beneath the shelf, relatively warm salty water causes basal melting, and the figure highlights the grounding line, basal hydrology, and the way ice flow feeds the shelf. Iceberg calving and surrounding sea ice are also shown, along with contrasting colder and warmer ocean waters.\n\nFor Greenland, the figure focuses more on direct surface melt and marine-terminating outlet glaciers. Atmospheric rivers, anticyclonic circulation, and adiabatic warming appear to enhance melt, producing runoff and meltwater at the margin. At the glacier front, calving and submarine melting are shown, driven by warmer Atlantic deep water. Overall, Antarctica appears dominated by ice-shelf processes, while Greenland seems more influenced by surface melt and fjord-ocean interactions.",
